, James earns $900 per week.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any calculation errors. First job: 20 hours * $20/hour = $400? Wait, hold on, no. Wait, 20 hours multiplied by $20 per hour is $400. Wait, but earlier I said 20 times 30 is 600. That must have been wrong. Oh no! There's a mistake here. I must have miscalculated earlier.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me re-examine. James earns $20 an hour while working his main job. So, if he works 30 hours, then 30 times $20. Wait, 30 times 20... Let me compute that again. 30 times 10 is 300, 30 times 20 is 300 + 30*10? No, wait. Wait, 20*30 is indeed 600. So, I must have misread earlier. If he works 30 hours at $20 an hour, that's $600. Then, for the second job, he works half that much, which is 15 hours. So, 15 hours times $20 per hour is another $300. So total is 600 + 300 = 900. So, $900 is total earnings. But wait, maybe I confused the second job with something else?\n\nWait, but let me check again, maybe I miscalculated the first part. So 20 times 30 is indeed 600. Yeah, that sounds right. So, there's no mistake there. Then why did I get confused before? Because I thought of 20*30 as $400, which was wrong. So, original problem says he earns $20 per hour while working at his main job. So 20 hours * 30 hours? No, 20 hours at 1 hour per hour, so 20 times 30. So, that's $600. So, second job is half at 30 hours. Therefore, 15 hours * 20 is 300. So total is 600 + 300 = 900. Therefore, total earnings per week is $900. So, final answer is \\boxed{900}?\n\nWait, just to make sure there's no other information. The problem says he works 30 hours at his main job and half that (so 15) at his second job. So addition is correct, no other jobs mentioned. Also, the percentages for the second job: 20% less? Wait, but the second problem says he earns 20% less while working his second job. But in my calculation, I didn't include that 20% loss? Wait, hold on! Wait, maybe I made a mistake here. The problem says: James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job. He earns 20% less while working his second job. So, does 20% less mean he earns 80% of his previous wage? Which would be 20% less. So for the second job, he earns 80% of $20 per hour. So, maybe I need to recalculate that?\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, perhaps the question is phrased that \"he earns 20% less while working his second job.\" So, does that mean his hourly rate is 20% less than his main job? So, if his main job is $20/hour, then his second job rate is 20% less. So, 20% less would be 80% of the original rate. Which is $20 * 0.80 = $16 per hour? Then, he works half of 30 hours at this rate. So, 15 hours times $16 per hour. Let me compute that. 15 * 16. 10*16=160, 5*16=80, so total is 240. Then total earnings would be main job 600 + second job 240 = 840? But then why did I previously think that the second job is 20% less? Wait, this is conflicting with my previous answer. So now I need to check which part is correct.\n\nWait, let me read the problem again: \"James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job. He earns 20% less while working his second job. He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.\"\n\nSo, the first sentence says he earns $20 per hour at his main job. The second sentence: earns 20% less while working his second job. So, this means that his second job rate is 20% less than $20? Or is it that he earns 20% of the main job? The wording is \"he earns 20% less while working his second job.\" This is ambiguous. Usually, when something says \"earn 20% less than X\", it means X times 80%, so 80% of X. So, if his main job is $20/hour, his second job would be 20% less, so 0.8 * 20 = $16/hour. Then, he works half as many hours at his second job. So, half of 30 hours is 15 hours. Therefore, 15 * 16 = $240. Then main job is 30 * 20 = $600. Total is 600 + 240 = $840. So, $840.\n\nBut earlier, I had considered 20% less as 20% more, which is inconsistent. So which is correct? The problem says \"he earns 20% less\". In math problems, unless specified otherwise, \"earn X less\" usually means that X is subtracted. So, if he earns $20 an hour, and he earns 20% less, meaning 80% of $20, which is $16/hour. Therefore, the second job is 0.8 * 20 = $16/hour. Therefore, the second job earning is 15*16 = 240. So total earnings 600 + 240 = 840.\n\nBut earlier, in my first calculation, I thought that 20% less would just mean 20% of the main job, but that's not correct. The 20% less is a percentage of the original rate. Therefore, the correct calculation is 840. So my initial mistake was not considering the percentage on the original rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $840, which would be boxed as \\boxed{840}.\n\nBut then why did I originally think 600 + 300 = 900? Because I thought the second job rate is 20% of the first job, which is not how it's phrased. The problem states he earns 20% less while working his second job. Therefore, 80% of $20 is $16. So, let me check once more with exact conversion.\n\nFirst job: $20/hour * 30 hours = $600.\n\nSecond job: original rate is $20/hour, 20% less means 80% of 20 = $16/hour. Worked 15 hours, so $16 * 15 = $240.\n\nTotal: 600 + 240 = $840.\n\nTherefore", "source_record": {"question": "James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?", "answer": "James earns 20*.2=$<<20*.2=4>>4 less while working his second job\nSo he earns 20-4=$<<20-4=16>>16 an hour\nAt his first job he earns 20*30=$<<20*30=600>>600\nHe works 30/2=<<30/2=15>>15 hours at his second job\nSo he earns 15*16=$<<15*16=240>>240\nSo he earns 600+240=$<<600+240=840>>840 a week\n#### 840", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["840"], "solution": "840"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 7801, 63759, 400, 17, 15, 458, 6460, 1393, 3238, 518, 806, 1887, 2618, 13, 220, 1260, 63759, 220, 17, 15, 4, 2686, 1393, 3238, 806, 2086, 2618, 13, 220, 1260, 4278, 220, 18, 15, 4115, 518, 806], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1753, 7801, 63759, 817, 2003, 13, 2055, 11, 1156, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 1438, 1495, 806, 17059, 16107, 1119, 806, 1887, 2618, 323, 806, 2086, 2618, 13, 6771, 752, 1191, 448, 279, 1887, 2618, 13, 1260, 63759, 400, 17, 15, 458, 6460, 11, 323, 566, 4278, 220, 18, 15, 4115, 1052, 13, 2055]}
