ip** that does **not** include that stop.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Problem\n\nKona drives:\n\n1. **9 miles** to the bakery.\n2. **24 miles** from the bakery to his grandmother's house.\n3. **27 miles** back from his grandmother's house to his apartment.\n\nSo the **total round trip** (including the bakery stop) is:\n\n$$\n9 \\text{ (to bakery)} + 24 \\text{ (from bakery to grandma)} + 27 \\text{ (back to apartment)} = 9 + 24 + 27 = 60 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\nThe **round trip without the bakery stop** would be:\n\n$$\n24 \\text{ (to grandma)} + 27 \\text{ (back to apartment)} = 24 + 27 = 51 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the Additional Miles\n\nThe additional miles driven in the round trip that includes the bakery stop compared to without it is:\n\n$$\n60 - 51 = 9\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{9}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "From his apartment, Kona drives 9 miles to the bakery.  From there, he drives 24 miles to his grandmother’s house.  From her house, he drives 27 miles straight to his apartment.  How many additional miles did Kona drive round trip to the bakery stop, compared to a round trip without the bakery stop?", "answer": "With the bakery stop, Kona drove a total of 9 + 24 + 27 = <<9+24+27=60>>60 miles.\nWithout the bakery stop, Kona drove a total of 27 + 27 = <<27+27=54>>54 miles.\nWith the bakery stop, Kona drives an additional 60 - 54 = <<60-54=6>>6 miles.\n#### 6", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: From his apartment, Kona drives 9 miles to the bakery.  From there, he drives 24 miles to his grandmother’s house.  From her house, he drives 27 miles straight to his apartment.  How many additional miles did Kona drive round trip to the bakery stop, compared to a round trip without the bakery stop?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["6"], "solution": "6"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 